# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository. The build matrix spans
# ubuntu-latest, ubuntu-24.04-arm, windows-latest and macos-latest, so a file
# committed with CRLF from a Windows checkout otherwise reaches the Linux legs
# with stray carriage returns. The index is already uniformly LF, so this
# codifies the status quo rather than renormalising anything.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells are the exception: cmd.exe mis-parses labels and goto targets
# in a batch file that uses LF endings.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never line-ending-converted, never textually diffed or merged.
# resources/lumoin-vericula-nuget-logo.png is packed into every NuGet package
# (see Directory.Build.props), so corrupting it silently ships broken packages.
#
# These are deliberately NOT tracked by Git LFS. The repository's entire binary
# payload is three files totalling ~130 KB, far below the size where LFS pays
# for itself, and LFS would carry a real cost here: actions/checkout in
# .github/workflows/main.yml does not set `lfs: true`, so an LFS-tracked icon
# would arrive in CI as a ~130-byte pointer file and get embedded verbatim into
# every published package. If genuinely large assets (fonts, video, datasets)
# are added later, introduce LFS together with `lfs: true` on the checkout step.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.snk binary
*.pfx binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary

# Fonts are binary too. None are committed today; these rules mean the first one
# added is handled correctly without anyone having to remember to do it.
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.eot binary

# SVG is XML, so it stays text and stays diffable.
*.svg text eol=lf
